TryHackMe: Walking an Application
Platform: TryHackMe
Room: Walking an Application
Difficulty: Easy
Date: 2026
Objective
Learn how to manually explore a web application to identify functionality, map attack surfaces, and discover potential security weaknesses without relying on automated tools.
Step 1: Understanding Application Mapping
The room introduces “walking an application” as the process of manually browsing a web app to understand how it works.
Key goals:
Identify pages, endpoints, and inputs
Understand user roles and functionality
Map navigation flow
Discover hidden or less obvious features
This helps build a full picture of the application before testing for vulnerabilities.
Step 2: Identifying Entry Points
While walking through the application, analysts look for input areas that could be abused.
Common entry points include:
Login forms
Search bars
URL parameters
File upload fields
Comment or feedback sections
Each input is a potential attack surface for injection or manipulation.
Step 3: Inspecting the Structure
The room emphasizes examining how the application is built and how data flows.
Things to observe:
URL structure (e.g., /profile?id=1)
Cookies and session handling
Client-side vs server-side processing
Hidden directories or pages
Source code comments or exposed scripts
This helps reveal weak points in the application design.
Step 4: Discovering Hidden Content
Applications often contain content not directly visible through navigation.
Techniques used:
Manually changing URL parameters
Checking for hidden admin pages
Reviewing robots.txt
Inspecting page source code
Testing directory paths
These methods help uncover functionality that may not be properly secured.
Step 5: Security Perspective
Walking an application is a foundational skill in web security because it helps identify:
Broken access control
Information disclosure
Weak authentication flows
Unprotected endpoints
Misconfigured services
Understanding structure is essential before attempting exploitation.
Step 6: Outcome
By completing this room, I learned how to manually explore and map a web application to identify its structure, inputs, and potential attack surfaces.
Lessons Learned
Manual application exploration is critical before automated scanning
Every input field can be a potential vulnerability
Hidden content often reveals security flaws
Understanding application flow improves vulnerability detection
Attack surface mapping is the first step in web security testing
Tools Used
Browser inspection tools
URL manipulation
View source / developer tools
Manual navigation techniques
